	Debugging

Levels and counters
-------------------

	./rvm -v			debug logging
	./rvm -q			warnings and errors only
	./rvm --stats			counters on exit

--stats prints instructions retired, wall time, throughput in MIPS, trap
counts split into ecalls, SBI calls and unsupported SBI calls, RAM and MMIO
load and store counts with fault counts, TLB hit, miss and walk counts, and
UART transmit and receive counts. Read it before optimising anything. A TLB
walk count that tracks the instruction count means the guest is running with
translation on and missing every time.

Build with MODE=debug when the disassembly has to line up with the source:

	make MODE=debug -j"$(nproc)"


The instruction trace
---------------------

--trace logs every retired instruction with its PC, encoding, privilege level
and satp. On a real boot that is gigabytes, which is why nobody uses it.

RVM_TRACE_FROM, or --trace-from on the command line, opens the gate at one
guest PC. Everything before it is dropped and everything from it on is logged:

	RVM_TRACE_FROM=0x80200000 ./rvm -k vmlinux -d disk.img
	./tools/run.sh --trace-from 0x80200000

The format is

	000080200abc: 00b50533  priv=1 satp=8000000080002000

A 16 bit encoding is padded so the columns line up. Both forms imply --trace
and raise the log level, so you do not have to pass them separately.


pc = 0xfffffffffffffffe
-----------------------

The guest jumped to data. -2 is what a jalr or a corrupted return address gives
you when it lands two bytes below zero, and it always means the emulator
retired something the guest never intended as code. Usually a decode bug that
got the instruction length wrong, or a trap path that wrote epc from a stale
npc.

Work it in this order.

1. Find how far it gets. Run with -n and bisect:

	./rvm -k vmlinux -d disk.img -n 1000000 --stats
	./rvm -k vmlinux -d disk.img -n 10000000 --stats

	The last -n that still prints sane output bounds the failure.

2. Trace the tail of it. Take a PC a few thousand instructions before the
   failure and open the gate there:

	RVM_TRACE_FROM=0x8021abcd ./rvm -k vmlinux -d disk.img > /tmp/t.log

   The last few lines of /tmp/t.log are the interesting ones.

3. Read the last retired instruction. If its length is wrong, the PC after it
   is wrong, and that is the bug. Check the encoding against the spec by hand;
   a compressed instruction that was treated as 32 bit is the common case.

4. Resolve the address in the guest:

	objdump -d vmlinux --start-address=0x8021ab00 \
		--stop-address=0x8021ac00
	addr2line -e vmlinux -f -C 0x8021abcd

   If addr2line names something that is not code, the guest really did jump
   into data and the fault is in the emulator's control flow, not in the
   decoding.

5. If the trace shows a trap instead, look at cause and tval. The trap path
   writes epc from npc, and mret and sret have to have their result copied
   into npc or the return address is stale by one instruction.

Every decode and trap bug found so far came out of this recipe.


Debugging the emulator itself
-----------------------------

	make MODE=debug -j"$(nproc)"
	gdb --args ./rvm -k vmlinux -d disk.img
	./tools/run.sh --gdb

Useful breakpoints are cpu_step, cpu_exec32, cpu_trap, mmu_translate and
bus_load. break cpu_trap if c->last_cause == 12 catches a guest page fault.

Note that gdb steps the emulator, not the guest. There is no guest stub.


Sanitizers
----------

	make MODE=sanity test TEST_ARGS=-v

That is ASan and UBSan at -O1 with the runtime linked in. CI runs it with

	ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=1:abort_on_error=0
	UBSAN_OPTIONS=print_stacktrace=1:halt_on_error=1

and it has found real bugs, including signed overflow in addw and subw and a
left shift of a negative value in sext(). Both are undefined in C and defined
in RISC-V, which is the combination that produces silently wrong results on
one compiler and crashes on another.

Run the guest under the sanitizers too, not just the tests:

	make MODE=sanity -j"$(nproc)"
	./rvm -k vmlinux -d disk.img -n 10000000


A standalone reproducer
-----------------------

The harness is a library, so a single failing case can be driven from its own
file without the whole suite:

	cat > /tmp/dbg.c <<'EOF'
	#include "fixtures/harness.h"
	#include "test.h"
	int main(void) {
	    th t;
	    th_init(&t);
	    th_emit(&t, ADDI(X_A0, X_ZERO, 1));
	    th_run_all(&t);
	    printf("x10 = %llx pc = %llx\n",
	           (unsigned long long)t.cpu.x[X_A0],
	           (unsigned long long)t.cpu.pc);
	    th_free(&t);
	    return 0;
	}
	EOF

	cc -std=c11 -D_POSIX_C_SOURCE=200809L -D_DEFAULT_SOURCE -O0 -g \
		-Isrc -Itests -o /tmp/dbg /tmp/dbg.c \
		build/tests/fixtures/harness.o build/librvm.a -lm

Run make test first so build/librvm.a exists. Under gdb this is much faster
than rebuilding the suite for every attempt.

For FDT problems there is a second trick: dump the blob and walk it outside the
emulator.

	./rvm --dump-dtb /tmp/rvm.dtb
	dtc -I dtb -O dts /tmp/rvm.dtb

If dtc can read it, the blob is well formed and the bug is in what the guest
does with it. If dtc cannot, the bug is in src/loader/fdt.c.


Device problems
---------------

virtio is the usual suspect. Turn on debug logging and watch the transport:

	./rvm -v -k vmlinux -d disk.img

Things worth checking by hand. The guest read MagicValue as 0x74726976 and
Version as 2. DeviceID is non zero for the slot the guest probed. QueueNumMax
was returned before the guest wrote QueueNum. The descriptor chain is walked in
the right direction: data is readable for T_OUT and carries VRING_WRITE for
T_IN, and the last descriptor is one writable status byte. The used ring len
counts every device written byte including the status byte.

An empty slot has to decode as no device present, meaning DeviceID reads 0. If
it faults instead, the guest probe dies and nothing after it works.

For interrupts, check that the device's IRQ number in the DTB matches
RVM_VIRTIO_IRQ(slot), and that plic_update() actually reports the source. The
guest masking it through sstatus or sie is a guest problem, not an emulator
one.

If the guest hangs in wfi, the interrupt it is waiting for never arrived. The
run loop refreshes mip every 256 instructions and immediately when a device
raises a line, so a hang here means the line was never raised at all.


Things that look like emulator bugs and are not
-----------------------------------------------

An all zero register file after a test block. th_run_all() does not stop on
STEP_TRAP, so the guest trapped and looped. Check last_cause.

Wrong results only for negative immediates. th_li() zero extends to 32 bits.

A test that passes locally and fails in CI. Check whether it depends on
uninitialised memory or on host timing, and run it under MODE=sanity.

No output at all from the test binary. It crashed and stdout was buffered. Use
stdbuf -o0 -e0.
